;margin-top:14px;}.cp-pearl b{color:var(--rn-text,#1E2640);}.cp-sysgrid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(255px,1fr));gap:11px;}.cp-sys{border:1px solid var(--rn-border,#E2E6F0);border-left:4px solid;border-radius:11px;padding:12px 14px;background:var(--rn-card,#fff);}.cp-sys.tier-red{border-left-color:var(--rn-red,#C93535);}.cp-sys.tier-amber{border-left-color:var(--rn-amber,#B86B00);}.cp-sys.tier-green{border-left-color:var(--rn-green,#1F8B5E);}.cp-sys-h{font-family:var(--mono,'SF Mono',SFMono-Regular,ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace);font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.05em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--rn-text,#1E2640);margin-bottom:9px;}.cp-sys-dx{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px;margin-bottom:8px;}.cp-sys-d{font-size:12px;line-height:1.45;color:var(--rn-sub,#5A6480);}.cp-dx{display:inline-block;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:600;text-decoration:none;padding:3px 10px;border-radius:999px;background:var(--rn-blue-bg,#EBF2FF);color:var(--rn-blue,#3B7DD8);border:1px solid transparent;transition:all .13s;}.cp-dx:hover{border-color:var(--rn-blue,#3B7DD8);}.cp-dx-off{background:#F1F3F8;color:var(--rn-sub,#7A8396);}.cp-hand{display:flex;gap:10px;background:var(--rn-blue-bg,#EBF2FF);border:1.5px solid #B0CCEF;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--rn-text,#1E2640);margin-top:26px;}.cp-hand b{color:var(--rn-blue,#3B7DD8);}.cp-mapic{font-size:18px;line-height:1;}.cp-flow{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;text-align:center;}.cp-dn{color:var(--rn-border,#C7CEDB);font-size:13px;line-height:1;margin:5px 0;}.cp-node{border-radius:11px;padding:10px 18px;font-weight:700;font-size:13.5px;max-width:560px;background:var(--rn-card,#fff);border:1.5px solid var(--rn-border,#E2E6F0);color:var(--rn-text,#1E2640);}.cp-node.start{background:var(--rn-blue-bg,#EBF2FF);border-color:#B0CCEF;color:var(--rn-blue,#3B7DD8);font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.06em;}.cp-node.danger{background:var(--rn-red-bg,#FDEAEA);border-color:#EDBFBF;color:var(--rn-red,#C93535);}.cp-node-sub{font-weight:500;font-size:10.5px;margin-top:3px;opacity:.9;}.cp-flowlabel{font-size:11px;color:var(--rn-muted,#9AA4B2);font-style:italic;margin:6px 0;}.cp-node.decision{background:var(--rn-amber-bg,#FEF5E4);border-color:#E8CE93;color:var(--rn-amber,#B86B00);}.cp-node.work{background:var(--rn-blue-bg,#EBF2FF);border-color:#B0CCEF;color:var(--rn-blue,#3B7DD8);font-weight:800;}.cp-node.final{background:var(--rn-blue-bg,#EBF2FF);border-color:#B7CEF0;color:var(--rn-blue,#3B7DD8);}.cp-branchbox{width:100%;border:1.5px dashed var(--rn-border,#D8DEEA);border-radius:14px;padding:14px 12px;background:transparent;}.cp-branchcols{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center;gap:10px;}.cp-col{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;flex:1 1 128px;max-width:150px;}.cp-feat{font-size:11px;font-weight:600;background:#F1F3F8;border:1px solid var(--rn-border,#E2E6F0);border-radius:8px;padding:6px 8px;width:100%;min-height:42px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;color:var(--rn-sub,#5A6480);}.cp-cdn{color:var(--rn-border,#C7CEDB);font-size:10px;margin:3px 0;}.cp-dx-node{font-size:12px;font-weight:800;border-radius:8px;padding:7px 8px;width:100%;border:1px solid;text-decoration:none;}.cp-dx-node.tier-red{background:var(--rn-red-bg,#FDEAEA);border-color:#EDC6C6;color:var(--rn-red,#C93535);}.cp-dx-node.tier-amber{background:var(--rn-amber-bg,#FEF5E4);border-color:#EAD4A4;color:var(--rn-amber,#B86B00);}.cp-dx-node.tier-green{background:var(--rn-green-bg,#E6F6EF);border-color:#B9E2CD;color:var(--rn-green,#1F8B5E);}.cp-test{font-size:10.5px;color:var(--rn-sub,#7A8396);font-weight:600;margin-top:5px;}.cp-dxlink{display:block;text-decoration:none;cursor:pointer;}.cp-dxlink:hover{box-shadow:0 0 0 2px currentColor inset;}</style></style><div class=\"cp-lead\">The <b>bone profile</b> — <b>calcium, phosphate, ALP</b> and <b>albumin</b> — is the first-line panel for calcium disorders, metabolic bone disease and monitoring. The single most important rule: <b>always correct the calcium for albumin</b> before interpreting it.</div><div class=\"cp-step\"><div class=\"cp-step-h\"><span class=\"cp-num\">1</span><span class=\"cp-title\">What it measures</span></div><div class=\"cp-step-body\"><div class=\"cp-killgrid\"><div class=\"cp-kill\" style=\"--kc:var(--rn-blue,#3B7DD8);--kcb:var(--rn-blue-bg,#EBF2FF);\"><div class=\"cp-kill-top\"><span class=\"cp-kill-ic\">🦴</span><span class=\"cp-kill-name\">Calcium (Ca²⁺)</span></div><div class=\"cp-kill-desc\"><b>Total calcium</b> — about half is <b>bound to albumin</b>. The <b>ionised</b> fraction is physiologically active; report the <b>albumin-corrected</b> value.</div></div><div class=\"cp-kill\" style=\"--kc:#6D5BD0;--kcb:#ECE9FB;\"><div class=\"cp-kill-top\"><span class=\"cp-kill-ic\">🧪</span><span class=\"cp-kill-name\">Phosphate (PO₄)</span></div><div class=\"cp-kill-desc\">Regulated by <b>PTH</b> (lowers it), <b>vitamin D</b> and <b>FGF23</b>. Often moves <b>opposite</b> to calcium in PTH-driven disorders.</div></div><div class=\"cp-kill\" style=\"--kc:var(--rn-amber,#B86B00);--kcb:#FFF4E0;\"><div class=\"cp-kill-top\"><span class=\"cp-kill-ic\">📈</span><span class=\"cp-kill-name\">ALP</span></div><div class=\"cp-kill-desc\"><b>Alkaline phosphatase</b> — raised with <b>osteoblast activity</b> (bone formation/turnover) <b>and</b> in <b>cholestasis</b> (liver). Also physiologically high in <b>growth</b> and <b>pregnancy</b>.</div></div><div class=\"cp-kill\" style=\"--kc:#0F9B8E;--kcb:#E1F5F2;\"><div class=\"cp-kill-top\"><span class=\"cp-kill-ic\">🧬</span><span class=\"cp-kill-name\">Albumin</span></div><div class=\"cp-kill-desc\">Needed to <b>interpret the calcium</b> — a low albumin lowers the <b>total</b> calcium without changing the ionised level.</div></div></div></div></div><div class=\"cp-step\"><div class=\"cp-step-h\"><span class=\"cp-num\">2</span><span class=\"cp-title\">Corrected calcium</span></div><div class=\"cp-step-body\"><div class=\"cp-lead\" style=\"margin:0;\"><b>Corrected Ca (mmol/L) = measured Ca + 0.02 × (40 − albumin&nbsp;g/L)</b></div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;color:var(--rn-sub,#5A6480);margin-top:9px;\">A <b>low albumin</b> lowers the total calcium and can <b>mask true hypercalcaemia</b> (or falsely suggest hypocalcaemia). Correcting to an albumin of 40&nbsp;g/L avoids this. Where accuracy is critical (e.g. critical illness), measure <b>ionised calcium</b> directly.</div></div></div><div class=\"cp-step\"><div class=\"cp-step-h\"><span class=\"cp-num\">3</span><span class=\"cp-title\">Typical adult ranges (check local lab)</span></div><div class=\"cp-step-body\"><div class=\"cp-reads\"><span class=\"cp-read\"><b>Adjusted Ca 2.20–2.60 mmol/L</b></span><span class=\"cp-read\"><b>Phosphate 0.8–1.5 mmol/L</b></span><span class=\"cp-read\"><b>ALP 30–130 U/L</b></span><span class=\"cp-read\"><b>Albumin 35–50 g/L</b></span></div></div></div><div class=\"cp-step\"><div class=\"cp-step-h\"><span class=\"cp-num\">4</span><span class=\"cp-title\">Read the profile</span></div><div class=\"cp-step-body\"><div class=\"cp-clue\"><span class=\"cp-when\">↑ Corrected calcium</span><span class=\"cp-arrow\">&rarr;</span><span class=\"cp-then\">Mostly <b>primary hyperparathyroidism</b> (outpatient) or <b>malignancy</b> (inpatient). <a class=\"cp-dx\" href=\"#conditions/ENDOCRINOLOGY/Hyperparathyroidism%20%2F%20Hypercalcaemia\">Hypercalcaemia &rsaquo;</a></span></div><div class=\"cp-clue\"><span class=\"cp-when\">↓ Corrected calcium</span><span class=\"cp-arrow\">&rarr;</span><span class=\"cp-then\"><b>Vitamin D deficiency</b>, CKD, hypoparathyroidism. <a class=\"cp-dx\" href=\"#conditions/ENDOCRINOLOGY/Hypoparathyroidism%20%2F%20Hypocalcaemia\">Hypocalcaemia &rsaquo;</a></span></div><div class=\"cp-clue\"><span class=\"cp-when\">Isolated ↑ ALP</span><span class=\"cp-arrow\">&rarr;</span><span class=\"cp-then\">Check the <b>GGT</b>: raised → <b>hepatic/cholestatic</b>; normal → <b>bony</b> (Paget&rsquo;s, osteomalacia, mets, healing fracture). <a class=\"cp-dx\" href=\"#conditions/MSK%20%2F%20RHEUMATOLOGY/Metabolic%20Bone%20Disease\">Metabolic bone disease &rsaquo;</a></span></div></div></div><div class=\"cp-warn\"><span class=\"cp-warn-ic\">🚩</span><div><b>Severe hypercalcaemia</b> (corrected Ca <b>&gt;3.0 mmol/L</b>) or a symptomatic patient (confusion, dehydration, arrhythmia) is an <b>emergency</b> → IV <b>0.9% saline</b> rehydration, then an IV <b>bisphosphonate</b> (e.g. zoledronate) for hypercalcaemia of malignancy; treat the underlying cause (malignancy or primary hyperparathyroidism). <b>Severe hypocalcaemia</b> can cause <b>tetany, seizures and a prolonged QT</b>.</div></div><div style=\"border:1.5px dashed #9aa4bf;border-radius:10px;padding:13px 15px;margin:14px 0;background:rgba(154,164,191,.08);color:var(--rn-sub,#5A6480);font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">📷 <b>Image placeholder</b> — a bone-profile interpretation grid: calcium (corrected), phosphate, ALP and PTH across the common patterns (primary/secondary hyperparathyroidism, osteomalacia, Paget&rsquo;s, CKD-MBD).</div><div class=\"cp-pearl\">🎓 <b>Exam pearl.</b> <b>Always correct the calcium for albumin.</b> A raised <b>ALP</b> is <b>bone vs liver</b> — the <b>GGT</b> splits them (raised = hepatic, normal = bony). Interpret <b>calcium with phosphate and PTH together</b> (see the PTH tab).</div></div></div><div id=\"biochemistry-2-1\" class=\"tt-pane\" style=\"display:none\"><div style=\"padding:20px 22px\"><div style=\"border:1.5px dashed var(--rn-border,#E2E6F0);border-radius:12px;padding:20px 22px;background:var(--rn-card,#fff);\"><div style=\"font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:var(--rn-text,#1E2640);margin-bottom:6px;\">Calcium / PTH / Vitamin D Panel</div><div style=\"font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--rn-sub,#5A6480);\">📋 Full notes are being written — what it is, how it&rsquo;s ordered, how to interpret it, normal &amp; abnormal results with linked diagnoses, and an image atlas. <b>Coming soon.</b></div></div></div></div><div id=\"biochemistry-2-2\" class=\"tt-pane\" style=\"display:none\"><div style=\"padding:20px 22px\"><div style=\"border:1.5px dashed var(--rn-border,#E2E6F0);border-radius:12px;padding:20px 22px;background:var(--rn-card,#fff);\"><div style=\"font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:var(--rn-text,#1E2640);margin-bottom:6px;\">Bone Profile Patterns (Osteoporosis / Osteomalacia / Paget&#x27;s)</div><div style=\"font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--rn-sub,#5A6480);\">📋 Full notes are being written — what it is, how it&rsquo;s ordered, how to interpret it, normal &amp; abnormal results with linked diagnoses, and an image atlas. <b>Coming soon.</b></div></div></div></div></div></div>",
